== narration ==
# Take A, "the company talks to you": every voice speaks to the listener.
[picture A]
narrator: Here are two little desks, back to back, with a screen between them.
spoke: I'm writing it down. No peeking, Pin!
pin: I'm writing it down too. I'm not looking. You can check on me.
narrator: Then they hold up their notes, and look at both.
spoke: Are they the same? Look closely with us.
pin: Almost! One dot is different. That's good too. Now we can learn something.
[fuller A]
narrator [balance]: {C4|C four} asks for two ways to say everything. Here's the test: if you can rebuild one telling from the other, you only really have one.
spoke [opsci]: One way asks: did it hold? Is the box still up? The other asks: what did we predict, and did the prediction survive?
pin [opsci]: Neither answer gives you the other. Held doesn't say whether holding was expected.
narrator [twice]: Big changes are built twice, apart. When the two agree, that's evidence. When they don't, that's information.
spoke [onehand]: And checking twice with the same hand still counts as one check. You need a second hand.
pin [corrected]: Once, two instruments built apart disagreed. The disagreement was real, and it fixed the paper.
narrator [writers]: Even the shop's table tool writes every cell twice, in its row and in its column, and checks one against the other.
spoke: So Pin and I write it down apart. Same answer, good. Different answer, good too.

# Take B, "the lap reader": one close voice reads to you; Spoke and Pin
# mostly talk to each other, and turn to you only in a short aside.
[picture B]
narrator: Spoke writes it down. Pin writes it down, too. They do not look.
spoke: Done, Pin. Don't look yet.
pin: I wasn't. Done too.
narrator: Then they look at both.
spoke: One dot is different.
pin: Good. We learn a lot from one dot.
[fuller B]
narrator [balance]: Spread two said {C4|C four} asks for two ways to say everything. If either telling can be rebuilt from the other, the pair has collapsed, and the second is decoration.
narrator [opsci]: Operational asks: did it hold, did it need rework, is the box still up. Scientific asks: what was predicted, what was observed, did the prediction survive.
pin [opsci]: Held doesn't tell you whether holding was expected.
spoke [opsci]: And a right forecast doesn't tell you who got up at three in the morning.
narrator [twice]: Load-bearing changes are built twice, independently, and kept apart. Agreement is evidence. Disagreement is information.
pin [onehand]: A check run twice by one hand is one check. I keep that in mind on every page.
narrator [corrected]: When one instrument was built twice, the two disagreed once. Sixty-eight is four times seventeen, and the disagreement corrected the paper.
spoke [writers]: Your table tool writes both sides too, Pin.
pin [writers]: Row and column. The two writers. Then it reads one against the other.

